WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.853 --> 00:00:05.659
When you can't judge the answers, the quality of your questions is your expertise.

2
00:00:06.679 --> 00:00:10.650
A question set is a short, fixed list of questions per phase,

3
00:00:10.813 --> 00:00:14.179
whatever the field: a database, a cable or a robot.

4
00:00:15.228 --> 00:00:21.068
Five areas cover most of it: scope, assumptions, risks, estimates and dependencies.

5
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.473
Ask them in that order.

6
00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:26.126
Each builds on the one before.

7
00:00:27.473 --> 00:00:29.911
Scope: what exactly will we have at the end?

8
00:00:30.976 --> 00:00:32.938
And what is explicitly not included?

9
00:00:33.686 --> 00:00:37.018
That half matters most: misunderstandings live there.

10
00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:40.685
An assumption: true for now, without proof.

11
00:00:41.395 --> 00:00:44.692
Ask: what are we assuming that, if wrong, changes the plan?

12
00:00:45.698 --> 00:00:49.367
For example: the factory's network can carry the new data.

13
00:00:50.099 --> 00:00:54.197
Write assumptions down: each one is a risk until it is checked.

14
00:00:55.548 --> 00:00:57.568
A risk may happen and hurt the goal.

15
00:00:58.267 --> 00:00:59.823
Ask: what worries you most?

16
00:01:00.575 --> 00:01:02.398
What went wrong on similar jobs?

17
00:01:03.446 --> 00:01:05.234
Ask each expert separately.

18
00:01:05.923 --> 00:01:08.873
In a group, the most senior voice sets the tone.

19
00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:13.648
An estimate is a forecast of time or cost, not a promise.

20
00:01:14.354 --> 00:01:16.943
Ask: how sure are you, and what is it based on?

21
00:01:17.963 --> 00:01:20.540
And ask for a range rather than one number.

22
00:01:21.857 --> 00:01:25.526
A dependency: your work waits on something from someone else.

23
00:01:26.232 --> 00:01:28.762
Ask: what do you need from others, and by when?

24
00:01:29.779 --> 00:01:33.877
The grid operator needs the load data before it can size the cable.

25
00:01:34.605 --> 00:01:38.947
The MES supplier waits for an interface from the ERP team.

26
00:01:39.992 --> 00:01:43.127
Most delays outside your field are not technical.

27
00:01:43.905 --> 00:01:45.995
They are dependencies that nobody owned.

28
00:01:47.078 --> 00:01:51.815
Use the sets at the start, at every change and before each go/no-go.

29
00:01:52.517 --> 00:01:56.871
The same questions every time: then experts prepare the answers.

30
00:01:57.908 --> 00:02:02.772
The typical failure: asking once, in a kick-off, and filing the answers.

31
00:02:03.514 --> 00:02:07.810
End every planning session with: what did we assume today that nobody checked?

32
00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:14.217
A kick-off for an MES at a metal parts maker, with the supplier and the IT lead.

33
00:02:15.294 --> 00:02:18.695
Scope: at go-live, which lines are on the MES?

34
00:02:19.412 --> 00:02:22.071
Lines 1 and 2, not line 3.

35
00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.979
Assumption: machine data comes in through the existing network.

36
00:02:27.703 --> 00:02:29.050
You write: unchecked.

37
00:02:30.162 --> 00:02:34.876
Risk: elsewhere the master data was dirty: products and the sequence of steps.

38
00:02:35.608 --> 00:02:39.602
Estimate: clean-up takes four to eight weeks, depending on the state.

39
00:02:40.644 --> 00:02:45.659
Dependency: an ERP interface from the client's IT, by 1 March.

40
00:02:46.381 --> 00:02:49.911
Five questions, three new risks, one owner each.

41
00:02:51.222 --> 00:02:55.634
You can't check an estimate by judging the work, only by how it was made.

42
00:02:56.438 --> 00:03:00.513
Three tools: a range, a reference class and a second estimate.

43
00:03:01.557 --> 00:03:06.480
Kahneman and Tversky named the planning fallacy in 1979:

44
00:03:06.666 --> 00:03:08.616
you underestimate your own task,

45
00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.414
even when you know similar tasks ran late.

46
00:03:12.168 --> 00:03:13.085
Experts too.

47
00:03:14.096 --> 00:03:18.520
A range is a low and a high estimate, between 3 and 6 weeks.

48
00:03:19.222 --> 00:03:21.358
It shows how uncertain someone is.

49
00:03:22.378 --> 00:03:27.150
A three-point estimate adds a most likely value to optimistic and pessimistic.

50
00:03:28.187 --> 00:03:34.317
PERT, from 1958, for the US Navy's Polaris programme, weights them: optimistic,

51
00:03:34.456 --> 00:03:38.717
plus four times most likely, plus pessimistic, divided by six.

52
00:03:39.426 --> 00:03:43.037
A convention, not a law: it pulls towards the long tail.

53
00:03:44.361 --> 00:03:47.937
A reference class: similar past projects as a yardstick.

54
00:03:48.659 --> 00:03:51.016
Kahneman and Flyvbjerg recommend it.

55
00:03:52.087 --> 00:03:53.886
Instead of reasoning from the plan,

56
00:03:54.072 --> 00:03:57.683
ask: how long did the last five interfaces like this take?

57
00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:05.998
Flyvbjerg and Gardner report in 2023: of about 16,000 mostly large projects,

58
00:04:06.137 --> 00:04:12.662
only 8.5 percent hit budget and schedule, and only 0.5 percent the benefits too.

59
00:04:13.668 --> 00:04:16.501
A base rate like that beats a confident plan.

60
00:04:17.823 --> 00:04:22.316
A second estimate: ask another expert, without showing the first number,

61
00:04:22.513 --> 00:04:23.186
and compare.

62
00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:24.721
Close?

63
00:04:25.422 --> 00:04:25.736
Good.

64
00:04:26.484 --> 00:04:27.215
Far apart?

65
00:04:28.003 --> 00:04:31.544
Then the gap is the conversation: what do you see that makes it longer?

66
00:04:32.576 --> 00:04:36.883
Watch the language: three weeks if nothing goes wrong is the best case.

67
00:04:37.602 --> 00:04:41.352
An estimate unchanged for months is often not being updated.

68
00:04:42.407 --> 00:04:43.614
Experts argue.

69
00:04:44.292 --> 00:04:47.554
Some agile teams only forecast from their track record;

70
00:04:47.705 --> 00:04:50.120
others want a firm number for the contract.

71
00:04:50.898 --> 00:04:54.416
Your question: a forecast with its uncertainty, or a hope?

72
00:04:55.503 --> 00:04:58.568
A sharp question: what would make it take twice as long?

73
00:04:59.292 --> 00:05:02.369
If the answer comes instantly, put that in the range.

74
00:05:03.679 --> 00:05:09.171
The automation supplier estimates the PLC work for a robot cell: optimistic 6 weeks,

75
00:05:09.241 --> 00:05:11.714
most likely 8, pessimistic 16.

76
00:05:12.773 --> 00:05:17.649
The three-point estimate: 6, plus four times 8, plus 16 is 54.

77
00:05:18.369 --> 00:05:20.517
Divided by six: 9 weeks.

78
00:05:21.563 --> 00:05:25.266
Their last three cells took 9, 11 and 10 weeks.

79
00:05:26.022 --> 00:05:29.505
That reference class says 9 is at the optimistic end.

80
00:05:30.567 --> 00:05:34.944
So plan around 10 to 11 weeks, and ask: what makes this one faster?

81
00:05:35.999 --> 00:05:38.321
A real reason, such as reusing code?

82
00:05:39.088 --> 00:05:39.506
Fine.

83
00:05:40.203 --> 00:05:42.687
If not, put the longer figure in the plan.
